  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nMid level ridging along with a slightly drier airmass across S TX \nwill keep rain chances very low through mid week. However, a broad \nmid level trough draped north to south across the western gulf and a \nbroad surface low across the Bay of Campeche will continue to bring \na medium to high chance of showers and thunderstorms to the Middle \nTexas Coastal Waters through mid week. \n\nThe forecast becomes more tricky by mid to late in the week due to \nthe uncertainty with the possible development of a tropical system \nin the Bay of Campeche. There is now a 80% chance of tropical \ndevelopment over the next 7 days. If it does not develop, then S TX \nwill likely have deeper moisture advection resulting in a more \nactive daily sea breeze through next weekend, with a low to medium \nchance of convection and near to slightly above normal temperatures \nthe latter half of the week. \n\nHowever, if a tropical system does develop, it will likely stay east \nof S TX, keeping S TX on the drier side. This is due a mid level \nwest to northwest flow ushering drier air across the region \nresulting in a low to very low chance of rain along, as well as \nhigher max temps. For now, will maintain the medium to high PoPs \nacross the coastal waters and low (10-20%) across the Coastal Bend \nand Victoria Crossroads the latter part of the week and into the \nweekend.\n\nThe main concern if a tropical system developing over the gulf will \nbe indirect impacts from an increased risk of coastal flooding, \nmoderate to high rip current risk and seas possibly building up to 9 \nfeet offshore. Currently a Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect \nthrough Tuesday evening due to a persistent northeast flow across \nthe northwest gulf waters pushing water up to the dunes during \ntimes of high tide. The Coastal Flood Advisory may need to be \nextended if a tropical system does develop.",
